Filtering with the `is` operator only works when the value is lowercase.

`.filter('email', 'is', 'NULL')` currently produces `where email is 'NULL'` (quoted string literal), which is invalid SQL — `IS` only accepts `TRUE` / `FALSE` / `NULL`. SQL keywords are case-insensitive, so uppercase values should work too.

Repro:
```ts
new Query().from('users', 'public').select().filter('email', 'is', 'NULL').toSql()
// actual:   select * from public.users where email is 'NULL';
// expected: select * from public.users where email is null;
```

Same for `NOT NULL`, `TRUE`, `FALSE`. Cause: `isFilterSql` in `packages/pg-meta/src/query/Query.utils.ts` compares the raw value against lowercase literals only.

## Which docs page?

[Login with Google](https://supabase.com/docs/guides/auth/social-login/auth-google) — the "Google pre-built" section, in the optional nonce step.

Source: `apps/docs/content/guides/auth/social-login/auth-google.mdx`

## Problem

The snippet that generates the nonce pair declares `hashedNonce` with `const` inside the `.then()` callback, so it is not reachable from the surrounding scope — but the comment directly below tells the reader to use it:

```js
const nonce = btoa(String.fromCharCode(...crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(32))))
const encoder = new TextEncoder()
const encodedNonce = encoder.encode(nonce)
crypto.subtle.digest('SHA-256', encodedNonce).then((hashBuffer) => {
  const hashArray = Array.from(new Uint8Array(hashBuffer))
  const hashedNonce = hashArray.map((b) => b.toString(16).padStart(2, '0')).join('')
})

// Use 'hashedNonce' when making the authentication request to Google
// Use 'nonce' when invoking the supabase.auth.signInWithIdToken() method
```

Running that verbatim gives:

```
ReferenceError: hashedNonce is not defined
```

Hoisting the variable on its own would not be enough either — `.then()` is asynchronous, so any code following the block runs before the digest resolves.

This one is easy to miss on a read-through because the code is correct in isolation; it only fails when you try to do the thing the next line asks for.

It also matters a little more than a typo would: the nonce binds Google's ID token to the request, so a reader who cannot get `hashedNonce` out of that block is likely to skip the nonce entirely, or pass the raw value to both sides — which is the same class of problem reported for the Apple guide in #48567.

## Suggested fix

The page already contains a correct version of this logic: the `generateNonce()` helper in the "One-tap with Next.js" example just below uses `await` and keeps both values in scope. Matching that here would make the two consistent:

```js
const nonce = btoa(String.fromCharCode(...crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(32))))
const encoder = new TextEncoder()
const encodedNonce = encoder.encode(nonce)
const hashBuffer = await crypto.subtle.digest('SHA-256', encodedNonce)
const hashArray = Array.from(new Uint8Array(hashBuffer))
const hashedNonce = hashArray.map((b) => b.toString(16).padStart(2, '0')).join('')

// Use 'hashedNonce' when making the authentication request to Google
// Use 'nonce' when invoking the supabase.auth.signInWithIdToken() method
```

I checked this on Node v25.9.0 and it produces a 64-character lowercase hex digest, as expected for SHA-256.

## One related thing

The HTML example a little above this shows `data-nonce=""` (empty), while the prose says the nonce must be provided in both `data-nonce` and the callback options. If it would help, I can include that in the same PR — happy to leave it out if you would rather keep the change narrow.

I would like to open the PR for this if that is useful.

