# Improve documentation

## Link

https://supabase.com/docs/guides/auth/oauth-server/oauth-flows

## Describe the problem

The code samples on this page do not match the shipped SDK. 

`@supabase/supabase-js` 2.112.2

There are 3 issues.

**1. `getUserGrants()` does not exist.**

Under "Viewing authorized applications" the docs say:

```typescript
const { data: grants, error } = await supabase.auth.oauth.getUserGrants()
```

The method is actually named `listGrants()`.  Running the documented sample throws `TypeError: supabase.auth.oauth.getUserGrants is not a function`.

**2. `revokeGrant()` takes an options object, not a positional string.**

Under "Revoking access" the docs say:

```typescript
const { error } = await supabase.auth.oauth.revokeGrant(clientId)
```

The real signature is:

```typescript
revokeGrant(options: { clientId: string }): Promise<AuthOAuthRevokeGrantResponse>
```

**3. The documented response shape does not match `OAuthGrant`.**

The docs show a flat object:

```json
[
  {
    "id": "grant-uuid",
    "client_id": "9a8b7c6d-5e4f-3a2b-1c0d-9e8f7a6b5c4d",
    "client_name": "My App",
    "scopes": ["email", "profile"],
    "created_at": "2025-01-15T10:30:00.000Z",
    "updated_at": "2025-01-15T10:30:00.000Z"
  }
]
```

The actual type nests the client and has a single `granted_at` timestamp:

```typescript
export type OAuthGrant = {
  /** OAuth client information */
  client: OAuthAuthorizationClient
  /** Array of scopes granted to this client */
  scopes: string[]
  /** Timestamp when the grant was created (ISO 8601 date-time) */
  granted_at: string
}

export type OAuthAuthorizationClient = {
  /** Unique identifier for the OAuth client (UUID) */
  id: string
  /** Human-readable name of the OAuth client */
  name: string
  /** URI of the OAuth client's website */
  uri: string
  /** URI of the OAuth client's logo */
  logo_uri: string
}
```

So `grant.client_name` and `grant.created_at` are both undefined. Anyone following the docs renders a list of blank app names. The real shape also
carries `uri` and `logo_uri`.

## Describe the improvement

Update the two code samples and the JSON block on the page.

Viewing authorized applications:

```typescript
const { data: grants, error } = await supabase.auth.oauth.listGrants()

if (error) {
  console.error('Error fetching grants:', error)
} else {
  console.log('Authorized applications:', grants)
}
```

Response:

```json
[
  {
    "client": {
      "id": "grant-uuid",
      "name": "My App",
      "uri": "https://example.com",
      "logo_uri": "https://example.com/logo.png"
    },
    "scopes": ["email", "profile"],
    "granted_at": "2025-01-15T10:30:00.000Z"
  }
]
```

Revoking access:

```typescript
const { error } = await supabase.auth.oauth.revokeGrant({ clientId })

if (error) {
  console.error('Error revoking access:', error)
} else {
  console.log('Access revoked successfully')
}
```

## Which docs page?

[Login with Apple](https://supabase.com/docs/guides/auth/social-login/auth-apple) — the **"Using sign in with Apple JS"** section.

Source: `apps/docs/content/guides/auth/social-login/auth-apple.mdx` (the `AppleIDSignInOnSuccess` example, around lines 203–225).

## Problem

The web example passes the **same raw nonce** to both `AppleID.auth.init()` and `supabase.auth.signInWithIdToken()`:

```ts
const nonce = crypto.randomUUID()

AppleID.auth.init({
  clientId: 'your-services-id',
  scope: 'name email',
  redirectURI: 'https://your-domain.com/auth/callback',
  usePopup: true,
  nonce: nonce,          // <-- raw
})

document.addEventListener('AppleIDSignInOnSuccess', async (event) => {
  const { data, error } = await supabase.auth.signInWithIdToken({
    provider: 'apple',
    token: event.detail.authorization.id_token,
    nonce: nonce,        // <-- raw
  })
})
```

Following this exactly always fails with:

```json
{"error":"invalid nonce","error_description":"Nonces mismatch"}
```

The reason is that the two sides expect different values:

- **Sign in with Apple JS** puts the value given to `init({ nonce })` into the id_token's `nonce` claim **verbatim** (it does not hash it).
- **GoTrue** compares the **SHA-256 of the nonce it receives** against the id_token's `nonce` claim. This is documented in `@supabase/auth-js`'s own type definitions:

  > `If the ID token contains a nonce claim, then the hash of this value is compared to the value in the ID token.`

So the comparison becomes `sha256(raw) === raw`, which can never hold.

## This contradicts the Flutter example on the same page

The Flutter example in the same document does it correctly (around lines 425–447):

```dart
final rawNonce = supabase.auth.generateRawNonce();
final hashedNonce = sha256.convert(utf8.encode(rawNonce)).toString();

final credential = await SignInWithApple.getAppleIDCredential(
  scopes: [...],
  nonce: hashedNonce,     // <-- hashed goes to Apple
);

final authResponse = await supabase.auth.signInWithIdToken(
  provider: OAuthProvider.apple,
  idToken: idToken,
  nonce: rawNonce,        // <-- raw goes to Supabase
);
```

The web example should follow the same pattern.

## Suggested fix

```ts
const rawNonce = crypto.randomUUID()

// SHA-256 hex, same as the Flutter example
const buf = await crypto.subtle.digest('SHA-256', new TextEncoder().encode(rawNonce))
const hashedNonce = Array.from(new Uint8Array(buf))
  .map((b) => b.toString(16).padStart(2, '0'))
  .join('')

AppleID.auth.init({
  clientId: 'your-services-id',
  scope: 'name email',
  redirectURI: 'https://your-domain.com/auth/callback',
  usePopup: true,
  nonce: hashedNonce,     // hashed goes to Apple
})

document.addEventListener('AppleIDSignInOnSuccess', async (event) => {
  const { data, error } = await supabase.auth.signInWithIdToken({
    provider: 'apple',
    token: event.detail.authorization.id_token,
    nonce: rawNonce,      // raw goes to Supabase
  })
})
```

## Note on the other web example

The first example in the same section (the `signInWithIdToken()` one, around lines 158–170) calls `AppleID.auth.signIn()` without showing an `init({ nonce })` call, yet still passes `nonce` to `signInWithIdToken()`. If `init()` was never given a nonce, the id_token carries no `nonce` claim and GoTrue skips the check — so that snippet happens to work, but passing `nonce` there is misleading. It may be worth aligning both examples.

## Environment

- `@supabase/supabase-js` 2.x (`@supabase/auth-js` 2.108.x)
- Sign in with Apple JS, `usePopup: true`
- Reproduced against a hosted Supabase project (Apple provider configured with a Services ID in *Client IDs*, no OAuth secret — the JS SDK path)
